[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":97},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":-5,"_42":43,"_44":45,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_54":36,"_55":56,"_57":58,"_59":36,"_60":61},"expiresAt",1795912848000,"id","skgo8xzuovrbxo6vigs2f589d","title","Q-Dir 和 DesktopOK 官方安装包遭篡改，投放 Python RAT","originalTitle","Q-Dir and DesktopOK Windows utilities compromised to push Python RAT","summary","Q-Dir 和 DesktopOK 的官方 SoftwareOK 分发渠道遭篡改，恶意安装包内含 Python RAT，影响新下载和既有安装更新；受影响的版本包括 Q-Dir 12.83.0.0、12.84.0.0、12.85.0.0，以及 DesktopOK 12.82.0.0、12.85.0.0，但并非所有版本均被感染。","reason","source",{"_73":96},"links",{"_61":95},"publishedAt","2026-11-28T15:32:49.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-28T23:32:36.497Z","timelineAt","category","activity","tags",[76,74,92,93,94],"score",63,"selected",false,"channel","news","story",{"_80":81,"_13":82},"x","readingMode","full","author","Bill Toulas","body",{"_88":-5,"_61":89,"_90":-5,"_91":36},"outline",[83],"relatedStories",[79],"topics",[68,69,70],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group",{"_62":63,"_64":65,"_66":67},"hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original","factId","fkbDkB0IG8Kc","reportCount",2,"additionalSourceCount",1,{"_71":77,"_73":78},{"_71":75,"_73":76},{"_71":72,"_73":74},"slug","malware","name","恶意软件","attack","攻击活动","supply-chain","供应链安全",{"_80":81,"_13":82},"publicId","78dedd98-a10e-469f-85b1-5badb1cccff8","Q-Dir与DesktopOK安装包投毒植入RAT",{"_11":84,"_85":86,"_87":65},"sec-1","text","Uncover email threats your existing defenses may miss.","level","zh","<p><img alt=\"Q-Dir and DesktopOK Windows utilities compromised to push Python RAT\" height=\"900\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/9aa351953e3cd98f.jpg\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/a92a23b9b05e7e55.jpg 720w, /dailyinfo/media/3493774b6bc14c48.jpg 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/9aa351953e3cd98f.jpg 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1600\"></p><p>Official distribution channels for the Q-Dir and DesktopOK Windows utilities were compromised to deliver trojanized installers containing Python-based remote access malware.</p><p>According to researchers at cybersecurity company AhnLab, the trojanized versions of the two software products are digitally signed using a certificate issued to their legitimate developer.</p><p>Q-Dir is a file manager promoted as a free, more powerful alternative to Windows File Explorer, while DesktopOK is a desktop icon-saving tool useful for when switching monitors or changing screen resolution.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.wiz.io/lp/ai-threat-readiness-playbook?utm_source=bleepingcomputer&amp;utm_medium=display&amp;utm_campaign=FY27Q2_INB_FORM_AI-Threat-Readiness-Playbook&amp;sfcid=701Vh00000cnU0lIAE&amp;utm_term=FY27Q4-bleepingcomputer-970x250-Nov&amp;utm_content=AITR-Playbook\"><img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/8f16a91279acf70b.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"image\"></a>\n</p><p>Nenad Hrg developed both tools and distributes them through the SoftwareOK website. While neither is a mainstream tool, they are used by IT professionals, power users, and system administrators.</p><p>Following <a href=\"https://www.majorgeeks.com/files/details/q_dir.html\">user complaints</a> dating back two weeks, AhnLab’s Security Intelligence Center (ASEC) confirmed that the installers for the two tools were compromised, affecting both new downloads and the updates for existing installations.</p><figure><img alt=\"Downloaded archive which contains the RAT\" height=\"509\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/e9f3c4cbba06ac56.jpg\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/6fc3e902f2f7bb19.jpg 720w, /dailyinfo/media/5cc918bf4413bda1.jpg 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/e9f3c4cbba06ac56.jpg 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"900\">\n<figcaption><strong>Downloaded archive which contains the RAT payload</strong><br>\n<em>Source: ASEC</em></figcaption>\n</figure><p>“Some Q-Dir and DesktopOK installation files distributed on the official SoftwareOK website had been tampered with to contain malicious files,” <a href=\"https://asec.ahnlab.com/en/96827/\">ASEC notes in their report</a>.</p><p>“Evidence indicates that malicious files were generated not only when users downloaded the programs directly from the official download page but also during the update process of previously installed legitimate programs.”</p><p>ASEC lists the following release versions as impacted:</p><ul>\n\t<li>Q-Dir versions 12.83.0.0, 12.84.0.0, and 12.85.0.0</li>\n\t<li>DesktopOK version 12.82.0.0 and 12.85.0.0</li>\n</ul><p>The security researchers said that not all listed versions were infected and shared SHA-256 hashes to help users distinguish legitimate from malicious installers.</p><p>However, ASEC warned that users who recently installed or updated one of the two trojanized tools should treat their system as potentially compromised, regardless of the version they use.</p><p>The researchers found that the Python RAT fetched by the malicious installers registers a scheduled task to help it persist on infected devices.</p><p>It supports shell command and Python code execution, file transfer, and can take screenshots. It also collects system information, including a list of security products active on the device.</p><p>ASEC says the malware contains a list of 1,206 processes matching various antivirus and endpoint detection and response (EDR) tools, both Korean and international, including AhnLab V3, CrowdStrike Falcon, SentinelOne, rend Micro, Avast, McAfee, ESET, Symantec, Kaspersky, Bitdefender, Sophos, Fortinet, Elastic Security, Malwarebytes, and FireEye.</p><p>This information is relayed to the command and control (C2) server to help malware operators plan their next actions without getting detected.</p><figure><img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/5339203bc5363c94.jpg\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/888330cec511760f.jpg 720w, /dailyinfo/media/ed1555874a4723e4.jpg 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/5339203bc5363c94.jpg 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"Attack chain\" height=\"344\" width=\"900\">\n<figcaption><strong>Attack chain</strong><br>\n<em>Source: ASEC</em></figcaption>\n</figure><p>ASEC could not determine how the installers were replaced or establish the timeframe during which the malicious versions were distributed.</p><p>Users are advised to check their downloads against the SHA-256 published on the official download page.</p><p>The researchers also say that removing a trojanized version of Q-Dir and DesktopOK does not ensure the elimination of the Python RAT, and users should check for suspicious Python execution environments and scheduled tasks along with traces of external network communication.</p><p>BleepingComputer has contacted Nenad Hrg to ask about the incident, but we have not received a response as of publication.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/lp/nb/email-scan?utm_source=display_network&amp;utm_medium=paid_display&amp;utm_campaign=2026_NA_Q4_Bleepingcomputer_Inadunit_October2026_scan&amp;utm_id=701Rd00000sqmWCIAY\">\n            <img src=\"/dailyinfo/media/1f6f9d6dc9ced8f5.jpg\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"article image\">\n        </a>\n    </p><h2 id=\"sec-1\">\n            <a href=\"https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/lp/nb/email-scan?utm_source=display_network&amp;utm_medium=paid_display&amp;utm_campaign=2026_NA_Q4_Bleepingcomputer_Inadunit_October2026_scan&amp;utm_id=701Rd00000sqmWCIAY\">Uncover email threats your existing defenses may miss.</a>\n        </h2><p>Adaptive Security's AI agents analyze email intent, context, and behavior to uncover phishing, business email compromise, and invoice fraud.</p><p>See what's getting through with a 14-day read-only scan of Microsoft 365 or Google Workspace, with no MX changes.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/lp/nb/email-scan?utm_source=display_network&amp;utm_medium=paid_display&amp;utm_campaign=2026_NA_Q4_Bleepingcomputer_Inadunit_October2026_scan&amp;utm_id=701Rd00000sqmWCIAY\">Start your free email security scan.</a>\n    </p>","zhKind","complete","供应链","后门","Microsoft","https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/q-dir-and-desktopok-windows-utilities-compromised-to-push-python-rat/","BleepingComputer",{"_98":99,"_100":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
